    "body": "## Motivation\n\n`SchedulePolicy._uncached_len` sizes a waiting request by its uncached tokens, and `_sort_by_hrrn` divides by that value when it builds the response ratio (`ratio_delta = waited_tokens / uncached`). The helper returns:\n\n```python\nreturn max(0, len(r.origin_input_ids) - r.num_matched_prefix_tokens)\n```\n\n`r.output_ids` is missing from the minuend, but `num_matched_prefix_tokens` is produced against `origin_input_ids + output_ids`: `_compute_prefix_matches` builds `prefix_ids = r.origin_input_ids + r.output_ids` and matches that, and `match_kv_cache` caps the result with `_compute_max_prefix_len(len(token_ids))`. The two sides of the subtraction therefore measure spans of different length.\n\nFor a fresh request `output_ids` is empty and the expression is correct, which is why this only shows up for requests that already generated tokens. It matters when such a request is retracted back into the waiting queue: `reset_for_retract` clears the matched prefix but keeps `output_ids` — it discards them only when `input_embeds is not None` — so those tokens have to be prefilled again. HRRN then underestimates the remaining work by `len(output_ids)`, which makes the response ratio too large and admits the request earlier than its real cost warrants.\n\nThe sibling helper `_shortest_prefill_work` in the same file already counts both spans:\n\n```python\nreturn max(1, len(r.origin_input_ids) + len(r.output_ids) - r.num_matched_prefix_tokens)\n```\n\nWhen the underestimation is large enough the clamped result reaches 0, and `_sort_by_hrrn` treats `uncached <= 0` as having no prefill work left (`return (-float(\"inf\"), rid)`), so the request is ordered ahead of the queue.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- `python/sglang/srt/managers/schedule_policy.py`: `_uncached_len` now measures by `r.seqlen`, which is defined as `len(origin_input_ids) + len(output_ids)`. One line changed; no new helper, no signature change, and no difference for requests whose `output_ids` is empty.\n\n- `test/registered/unit/managers/test_schedule_policy.py`: one regression test added to the existing `TestSchedulePolicyHRRN` class. It puts a retracted request (90 output tokens, 90 matched) and a fresh request (same origin length, same matched count) through `_sort_by_hrrn`; the retracted one has more work left and must sort second.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nNot applicable: the change only affects the order in which waiting requests are admitted. It does not touch model forward, KV cache contents, sampling, or logits.\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nNot applicable: this is a denominator in an integer sort key. It adds no work to the scheduling path.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Format code with pre-commit](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#format-code-with-pre-commit).\n- [x] Add unit tests according to the [Run and add unit tests](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#run-and-add-unit-tests).\n- [ ] Update documentation according to [Write documentations](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#write-documentations).\n- [ ] Provide accuracy and speed benchmark results according to [Test the accuracy](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#test-the-accuracy) and [Benchmark the speed](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#benchmark-the-speed).\n- [x] Follow the SGLang code style [guidance](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#code-style-guidance).\n\n## Review and Merge Process\n\n1. Ping Merge Oncalls to start the process. See the [PR Merge Process](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/main/.github/MAINTAINER.md#pull-request-merge-process).\n2. Get approvals from [CODEOWNERS](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/main/.github/CODEOWNERS) and other reviewers.\n3. Trigger CI tests with [comments](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.